{
  "id": "Compound::DB01181 - Disease::DOID:2531",
  "verbalization": "Ifosfamide is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hematologic Cancer through multiple mechanistic pathways. The evidence suggests that Ifosfamide, through its alkylating activity, contributes to the treatment of Hematologic Cancer, as it is included in the category of Antineoplastic Alkylating Agents, which are known to inhibit DNA synthesis and induce apoptosis in cancer cells. This is further supported by the relationship between Ifosfamide and Lomustine, both of which are alkylating agents, and their shared role in treating Hematologic Cancer. Additionally, the evidence indicates that Ifosfamide may contribute to the development of Hematologic Cancer through a mechanism involving the suppression of T-cell function, as suggested by the indirect pathway linking Ifosfamide to Premature Menopause, which is associated with Doxorubicin, a chemotherapy agent that also targets hematologic malignancies. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential of Ifosfamide as a therapeutic agent for Hematologic Cancer, particularly through its alkylating activity and its role in modulating immune function. The evidence, while not directly linking Ifosfamide to Hematologic Cancer, provides a plausible mechanistic framework that connects it to the disease through its pharmacological properties and its role in the broader context of chemotherapy and immunomodulation.",
